<section class="section">
  <div class="conteneur auto-prose">
    <h2>Reading the output</h2>
    <p>Each character of the text becomes one entry of the <code>tokens</code> list, in order. <code>codePoint</code> gives its Unicode identifier and <code>supported</code> says whether it is supported. <code>steps</code> lists the keys to send, in order: <code>code</code> is the physical key, <code>modifiers</code> the modifiers to hold (<kbd>AltGr</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd>), and <code>role</code> is <code>deadKey</code> for a dead key, to be followed by the next key.</p>
    <p>Example for “œ”:</p>
    <pre class="convertisseur__json" tabindex="0" aria-label="Sample output for the character œ"><code>{
  "index": 1,
  "codePoint": "U+0153",
  "character": "œ",
  "kind": "character",
  "supported": true,
  "method": {
    "kind": "direct",
    "preconditions": {
      "capsLock": "off"
    },
    "steps": [
      {
        "role": "character",
        "code": "KeyO",
        "modifiers": [
          "AltGr"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "recommendedForAutomation": true
  }
}</code></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="conteneur">
    <div class="auto-entete">
      <h2>From intent to application</h2>
      <p class="auto-entete__chapo">Robust keyboard automation keeps intent separate from the mechanics of the system that executes it.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="auto-chaine" role="list">
      <li class="auto-chaine__etape">
        <h3 class="auto-chaine__titre">1. Agent</h3>
        <p>The agent states the goal: type a character, complete a field or invoke a command. It should not assume that the rendered character tells it which physical key to send.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="auto-chaine__etape">
        <h3 class="auto-chaine__titre">2. Logical sequence</h3>
        <p>The specification describes physical positions and logical modifiers in a portable order. This layer can be verified and logged, and is independent of the driver you choose.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="auto-chaine__etape">
        <h3 class="auto-chaine__titre">3. Driver, then application</h3>
        <p>The driver translates the logical sequence for the operating system or hardware. The application then receives the events: the installed and active layout determines the resulting text.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="conteneur auto-prose">
    <h2>When to use this converter</h2>
    <p>Use it when a scenario must reproduce real typing on a machine configured with AZERTY Global: a <em>computer-use</em> agent, interface QA, an RPA robot, a demonstration station, a robotics test bench or a KVM. In all of these cases, the final text depends both on the active layout and on the driver that injects the events.</p>
    <p>Do not use it as a substitute for a text field, a business API, the clipboard or a semantic text entry already supported by your tool. Keystroke automation is appropriate when the exact behavior of the keyboard is part of what you are testing or controlling; otherwise, direct text entry is simpler and less brittle.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="conteneur auto-prose">
    <h2>The key model</h2>
    <p>The contract relies on <code>KeyboardEvent.code</code>: it identifies a physical position such as <code>KeyA</code> or <code>Digit2</code>, rather than deriving a key from the rendered character. Logical modifiers are explicit, including <kbd>AltGr</kbd>, and one sequence can contain several ordered steps.</p>
    <p>Dead keys need particular care. They produce an intermediate state, then compose with the following key. The converter therefore keeps the complete order of the sequence instead of reducing the intent to a single character. This is essential for accents, symbols and languages that rely on composition.</p>
    <p>The result is never universal: Windows, macOS, Linux, browsers, remote sessions, accessibility permissions and hardware drivers do not offer exactly the same injection capabilities. Always check the target OS, the installed layout and the currently active layout before running a critical scenario.</p>
  </div>
</section>

